  number = 16,
  pages = {11374-82},
  abstract = {Classical trajectory calculations were employed to study
             oxygenated (100) diamond surfaces. Atomic forces were computed
             from two types of potential energy functions, a quantum
             mechanical potential based on the semiempirical PM3 (parametric
             method number 3) Hamiltonian which was used to describe the
             central core of the surface model and an empirically parametrized
             potential that was developed to extend the size of the model
             surface. The results indicate that the energetically most
             favorable structure of an oxygenated surface is a bridge oxygen
             monolayer. However, due to the effect of entropy, an on-top
             oxygen monolayer dominates at high temperatures. Surfaces
             saturated with oxygen molecules were found to be unstable even at
             room temperature and to convert to the on-top atomic oxygen
